<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Solothurn</h1><p class="st-meta">1218 CE – 1798 CE (approx.) · capital Solothurn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Catholic city-republic on the Aare, a Swiss confederate from 1481 and for over two centuries the residence of the French ambassadors to Switzerland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Solothurn grew from the Roman settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salodurum">Salodurum</a> and the shrine of the martyrs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ursus-of-solothurn">Ursus</a> and Victor. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">Zähringen</a> dukes it was a town of growing importance, and when their line died out in 1218 it became an imperial city. It built a rural territory and allied closely with neighbouring Bern.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Solothurn was ruled by patrician families through councils. It fought with the Swiss in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a> and was admitted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-fribourg">Fribourg</a> in 1481. After a brief period of division, it remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. From 1530 to 1792 it was the seat of the French ambassadors, whose presence brought money, French culture and baroque buildings, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-ursus-cathedral">St. Ursus Cathedral</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French troops occupied Solothurn in 1798 and it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. Restored as a canton under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> in 1803, it remains a canton of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1218 CE – 1798 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.94%;width:11.54%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1218 CE – 1798 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Solothurn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Solothurn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1508&amp;focus=canton-of-solothurn" aria-label="Canton of Solothurn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4857 521 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Canton of Solothurn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5177 777L5186 782M5177 777L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5177 777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5177 777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Solothurn. <a href="/atlas?year=1508&amp;focus=canton-of-solothurn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
